ent 2's earlier answer) and Anna's comment. We need to consider all candidates. We have our own notes for A, B, C, D. We need to argue.\n\nWe can either maintain vote for B, or shift. Let's evaluate.\n\nCandidate B: strengths: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical). Concerns: weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation, communication). No info on decision quality, adaptability.\n\nCandidate A: strengths: 100% reliable, quick correct decisions, excellent attention, calm in crisis, puts concern for others above everything (crew cooperation). Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nCandidate C: strengths: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership. Concerns: not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized, regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas, unfriendly, eats unhealthily.\n\nCandidate D: strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: know-it-all, hot temper, no reliability evidence.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate best fits overall. Let's weigh.\n\nReliability: A (100% reliable), B (very conscientious), C unknown, D unknown.\n\nStress resilience: A (calm in crisis), B (handles stress well), C (anticipates dangerous situations), D (responds adequately).\n\nTechnical competence: A (understands complicated technology), B (weather assessment, computer skills), C (complex connections, spatial vision), D (solves problems extremely well, concentration).\n\nDecision quality: A (quick correct decisions), B unknown, C anticipates dangerous, D solves problems.\n\nAttention: A (excellent attention), B weak memory for numbers, C can be unorganized, D can concentrate very well.\n\nCrew cooperation: A puts concern for others above everything, but difficulty communicating, egocentric. B makes nasty remarks. C very good leadership but not good at criticism, show-off, unfriendly. D know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nCommunication: A difficulty communicating ideas, B nasty remarks, C not open to new ideas, D hot temper.\n\nResponsibility: A reliable, B conscientious, D takes responsibility seriously, C leadership.\n\nAdaptability: A not willing to further education, B unknown, C not open to new ideas, D unknown.\n\nOverall, A seems strong on reliability, decision, attention, crew concern, but communication issues. B strong on reliability, stress, technical, but attention and crew issues. C strong on hazard anticipation and leadership, but reliability unknown, unorganized, unfriendly. D strong on problem solving and responsibility, but no reliability, crew issues.\n\nWhich is best? Likely A or B. Need to compare A vs B. A has perfect reliability, quick decisions, excellent attention, calm in crisis, crew concern. B has good stress handling, conscientious, but weak memory and nasty remarks. A's communication difficulty and egocentric may affect crew, but he puts concern for others above everything, which may mitigate. B's nasty remarks are more severe for crew cooperation. Also B's weak memory for numbers could affect attention. So A may be stronger overall.\n\nThus I might shift my vote to Candidate A. Provide argument referencing earlier discussion: Anna supports C, Markus (me) originally B, Sofia supports A. I can now argue that A's reliability and decision quality outweigh B's concerns, and C's lack of reliability and unfriendly nature, D's unknown reliability. So I will change vote to Candidate A.\n\nWe need to reference explicit facts: A 100% reliable, quick correct decisions, excellent attention, calm in crisis, puts concern for others above everything. B's weak memory for numbers, nasty remarks. C's lack of reliability, unfriendly, unorganized. D's lack of reliability evidence